"""The measurement framing (DECISION-20261027-joseph-measurement-framing-and-limitation-scoping item 1): the two
abstracts (abstract blocks only; director's exception) and Sec. I of the paper. Drafts for the PRD read and Joseph."""
import re, sys
D = sys.argv[1]
HDR = "% Measurement framing (DECISION-20261027-joseph-measurement-framing-and-limitation-scoping.md item 1; director 2026-10-27): led by the sixteen-cell result; the three limitations stated once each; no configuration label; every number through \\fvval (values_fived16.tex). Draft for the PRD read and Joseph."
PA = (r"We report a simultaneous measurement of the $\nu_{\mu}$ charged-current inclusive cross section in five observables, the muon transverse and longitudinal momenta, the available hadronic energy, the three-momentum transfer and the hadronic invariant mass, from MINERvA medium-energy open data. "
      r"The data are unfolded without binning by OmniFold and reported in sixteen cells with their covariance, whose median per-cell uncertainty is \fvval{sigmed-C_total_resp}\%. "
      r"With this covariance and each prediction's finite-sample uncertainty, four predictions compared on all sixteen cells, among them the simulation's MINERvA-tuned prior, give $\chi^{2}$ between \fvval{g-chi2int-min16} and \fvval{g-chi2int-max16} for 16 degrees of freedom, and GiBUU, compared on its 8 cells, gives \fvval{g-gibuu-total-chi2int}. "
      r"The result has three limitations: the coverage of its intervals has not been validated; in a fake-data study, the reported uncertainty does not cover the estimator's bias at GiBUU-like truths or at a synthetic $q_{3}$ tilt; and the sixteen-cell $\chi^{2}$ values fall by \fvval{sens-flux-fall-lo}--\fvval{sens-flux-fall-hi}\% when only the flux correlations are weakened by \fvval{sens-alpha-pct}\%. "
      r"The same unfolding reproduces the integral of the published muon-kinematic cross section within about 1\%, with a correlated shape difference. "
      r"Pseudo-experiments measure a background-subtraction bias that negative-weight subtraction largely removes, a partial recovery of generator-sized truth changes, and statistical undercoverage of a purity-subtracted variant at the tested truths. "
      r"As an illustration, simulation-calibrated tests of hybrid null hypotheses for five generator predictions all reject at the 5\% familywise level, with no evidence of gain over matched coarse projections.")
NA = (r"This note documents an unbinned, simultaneous unfolding of MINERvA medium-energy $\nu_{\mu}$ charged-current inclusive open data with OmniFold, and its validation. "
      r"Its principal result is a cross section in five observables, muon transverse and longitudinal momentum, available energy ($\Eavail$), three-momentum transfer ($q_{3}$) and hadronic invariant mass ($W$), in sixteen cells, with a covariance of statistical, flux, interaction, detector, lateral, normalization, generator-shape and calorimetric-response terms; its median per-cell uncertainty is \fvval{sigmed-C_total_resp}\%. "
      r"Four generator predictions compared on all sixteen cells, among them the simulation's MINERvA-tuned prior, give $\chi^{2}$ between \fvval{g-chi2int-min16} and \fvval{g-chi2int-max16} for 16 degrees of freedom, and GiBUU, on its 8 cells, \fvval{g-gibuu-total-chi2int}. "
      r"The coverage of the result's intervals has not been validated; in a fake-data study, the reported uncertainty does not cover the estimator's bias at GiBUU-like truths or at a synthetic $q_{3}$ tilt; and the sixteen-cell $\chi^{2}$ values fall by \fvval{sens-flux-fall-lo}--\fvval{sens-flux-fall-hi}\% when only the flux correlations are weakened by \fvval{sens-alpha-pct}\%. "
      r"The note also gives the reproduction of the published double-differential cross section in muon momentum, whose integral agrees within about one percent, with a correlated shape difference and a covariance that has not been independently validated; three- and four-observable central values; and an earlier purity-subtracted five-observable central value on a finer grid, whose covariance does not meet its specification's acceptance criteria and whose quoted uncertainties omit that estimator's background bias of up to 4\% at high $W$ and its regularization bias. "
      r"The validation studies show that negative-weight background subtraction removes most of the purity method's bias, that generator-sized truth changes are recovered only in part, a residual interpreted as regularization bias, that statistical intervals of a purity-subtracted variant miscover at the tested truths, and that no tested reporting definition meets a predeclared 10\% precision criterion. "
      r"Joint tests calibrated in simulation reject hybrid null hypotheses for five generator predictions at a 5\% family-wise error rate; they are not physical exclusions, show no evidence of discrimination beyond matched coarse projections, omit an uncertainty on the calorimetric recoil response and assume an unshown convergence of the fine grid on which the null hypotheses are defined, and NuWro's total-test rejection is not robust to a hydrocarbon-target correction.")
def put_abstract(path, text):
    t = open(path).read().splitlines(keepends=True)
    a = next(i for i, l in enumerate(t) if l.strip() == r"\begin{abstract}")
    b = next(i for i, l in enumerate(t) if l.strip() == r"\end{abstract}")
    keep = [l for l in t[a + 1:b] if l.lstrip().startswith("%") or not l.strip()]
    keep = [l for l in keep if l.strip()]
    t[a + 1:b] = [HDR + "\n"] + keep + [text + "\n"]
    open(path, "w").write("".join(t))
put_abstract(D + "/main_paper.tex", PA)
put_abstract(D + "/main_note.tex", NA)
P = D + "/paper_body.tex"; t = open(P).read()
def pat(s): return re.compile(r"\s+".join(re.escape(w) for w in s.split()))
E = [
("I2", r"We apply the method to MINERvA medium-energy $\nu_{\mu}$ data from the MINERvA open-data release~\cite{MINERvA:OpenData}. We first reproduce the published double-differential charged-current inclusive cross section in transverse and longitudinal muon momentum, $d^{2}\sigma/(d\pT\,d\pPar)$~\cite{MINERvA:2021owq}, then add available hadronic energy $\Eavail$, three-momentum transfer $q_{3}$, and hadronic invariant mass $W$, and obtain simultaneous three-, four-, and five-observable central values.",
       r"We apply the method to MINERvA medium-energy $\nu_{\mu}$ data from the MINERvA open-data release~\cite{MINERvA:OpenData}. The result is a simultaneous charged-current inclusive cross section in five observables, the transverse and longitudinal muon momenta $\pT$ and $\pPar$, available hadronic energy $\Eavail$, three-momentum transfer $q_{3}$, and hadronic invariant mass $W$, reported in sixteen cells with its covariance (Sec.~\ref{sec:result16}) and compared with five generator predictions (Sec.~\ref{sec:gen16}). We first reproduce the published double-differential cross section in transverse and longitudinal muon momentum, $d^{2}\sigma/(d\pT\,d\pPar)$~\cite{MINERvA:2021owq}, as an end-to-end test of the unfolding, and we also give three- and four-observable central values."),
("I3", r"We evaluate this implementation as a case study of multidimensional unfolding and of inference with a biased estimator. The question is whether it gives reliable multidimensional cross sections when the simulation mismodels the truth by generator-sized amounts, and what validation is needed before its outputs can be interpreted.",
       r"The validation studies show how far the result can be trusted: the two-dimensional reproduction, the background subtraction, the recovery of generator-sized truth changes, a coverage test and a fake-data study of the estimator's bias. They ask whether the unfolding gives reliable multidimensional cross sections when the simulation mismodels the truth by generator-sized amounts."),
("I4", r"A calibration obtained in this way is valid to the extent that the simulated chain reproduces the analysis of the data.",
       r"A calibration obtained in this way is valid to the extent that the simulated chain reproduces the analysis of the data." "\n\n"
       r"The result has three limitations, each stated with it. The coverage of its intervals has not been validated. In a fake-data study at \fvval{f0b-all-truths} truths, the reported uncertainty does not cover the estimator's bias at the GiBUU-like truths or at a synthetic $q_{3}$ tilt (Sec.~\ref{sec:result16}). The generator $\chi^{2}$ depends on the flux correlations: weakening only them by \fvval{sens-alpha-pct}\,\% lowers the $\chi^{2}$ of the sixteen-cell predictions by \fvval{sens-flux-fall-lo}--\fvval{sens-flux-fall-hi}\,\% (Sec.~\ref{sec:gen16})."),
("I5", r"Throughout, a three- to five-observable result is a central value without intervals of validated coverage: the output of the estimators of Sec.~\ref{sec:method}, with the observable definitions, simulated detector response, background treatment, and regularization given there. The sixteen-cell result of Sec.~\ref{sec:result16} also has total uncertainties, whose coverage is not validated. The paper does not deliver a validated five-observable cross-section measurement.",
       r"The three- and four-observable results are central values without intervals of validated coverage: the output of the joint-test estimator of Sec.~\ref{sec:method}, with the observable definitions, simulated detector response, background treatment, and regularization given there. The sixteen-cell result of Sec.~\ref{sec:result16} also has total uncertainties, whose coverage is not validated."),
("I6", r"Sections~\ref{sec:repro2d}--\ref{sec:limits} test whether the unfolded results and their intervals can be validated. These validation studies are the paper's main results, and Table~\ref{tab:findings} lists each with its test. As a secondary application,",
       r"Sections~\ref{sec:repro2d}--\ref{sec:limits} give the validation studies that support the result and Sec.~\ref{sec:result16} the result itself; Table~\ref{tab:findings} lists each finding with its test. As an application,"),
("K1", r"The study's results are the validation findings summarized in Table~\ref{tab:findings}.",
       r"The paper's result is the sixteen-cell cross section of Sec.~\ref{sec:result16}, with its covariance and its generator comparison; the validation findings summarized in Table~\ref{tab:findings} show how far it can be trusted."),
]
for k, old, new in E:
    m = list(pat(old).finditer(t))
    if len(m) != 1: sys.exit(f"{k}: {len(m)} matches")
    t = t[:m[0].start()] + new + t[m[0].end():]
open(P, "w").write(t); print("framing applied")
